\section{Introduction}








Nowadays, stability is one of those things everyone is willing to achieve. Predictability, order, no chaos. And the behavior of any object can be expressed using functions. The simple functions we use on our daily basis are hardly ever stable: they output a different graph if we recursively apply a function inside itself. They also tend to behave chaotically if you repeat this process many times. Or do they? In this paper we will search for stability from the functions perspective and deduce how to find the stable intervals after applying the recursion. 


The main goal of this paper is to investigate in a general way the convergence of recursive functions and find their basins of attraction. We will develop a method that accomplishes this goal and follow up with $3$ examples to find useful applications.

The exponential family of functions will relate recursion to power towers. This will be helpful to study the convergence of power towers using our method. We will find out what is the result of raising a complex number to the power of itself infinitely many times: $z^{z^{z^{z^{\cdots}}}}$. Does it converge to an imaginary number and therefore achieve stability? Or does it behave chaotically forever? 


\subsection*{Definitions}
\begin{itemize}
\item Definition 1: The functions analyzed in this paper will be represented as $f(x)$, their domain as $D$ and their range as $R$. $f(x)$ will be continuous and differentiable on $D$. This condition rules out many unnecessary exceptions that exceed the aim of this paper.
\item Definition 2: Representation of a recursive composition of the same function - $f^0(x)=x$ and $f^{n}(x)=f(f^{n-1}(x))$.

\item Definition 3: Fixed point - For any function $f(x)$, a point satisfying $f(x_0)=x_0$.
\item Definition 4: Attracting point \cite{MonardCDynamics} - A fixed point $x_0$ is called attracting if there exists $\delta>0$ such that
for all $x$ satisfying $|x-x_0|<\delta$,
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty} f^n(x)=f(x_0)=x_0.
\]
\item Definition 5: Basin of attraction - The interval of values around $x_0$, such that for every value,
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty} f^n(x)=x_0.
\]

\item Definition 6: Tetration  - ${}^{n}k = \underbrace{k^{k^{\cdot^{\cdot^{k}}}}}_{n}$. The repeated exponentiation from top to bottom. It is very important not to confuse the order of exponentiation in tetration:
\[
\begin{cases}
{}^{3}k=k^{(k^{k})}\\
{}^{3}k\neq(k^{k})^{k}
\end{cases}
\]

For example, for $k=3$,

\[
\begin{cases}
{}^{3}3=3^{(3^{3})}=3^{27}\\
{}^{3}3\neq(3^{3})^{3}=3^9
\end{cases}
\]

\end{itemize}

\newtheorem{claim}{Claim}
\begin{claim}\label{bound1}
Let $x_0$ be a fixed differentiable point on the domain $D$. In order for $x_0$ not only to be a fixed, but also an attracting point, $x_0$ must satisfy ${|f'(x_0)|<1}$.
\end{claim}


This claim is proven in the appendix \ref{App1}. So the final condition for an attracting point is, \begin{equation}
\boxed{|f'(x_0)|<1}
