\subsection{Basin of attraction}

Now, let's focus on the attracting point case, when $|f'(x_0)|<1$. We will investigate what interval of values around $x_0$ that converges to $x_0$.


\subsubsection*{Proposition}
Let $f(x)$ be a function on the interval and domain $D$. If $|f'(x)|<1$, and there is a fixed point $x_0$, then $f(x)$ converges to a single point $x_0$. In other words,  $\lim_{n \to \infty}f^n(x)=x_0$.



\subsubsection*{Proof \cite{jachymski2024banach}}
\label{MainProof}

Let $x_1, x_2 \in D$ and $x_1<x_2$. Since $D$ is an interval and $f(x)$ is differentiable, by the Mean Value Theorem \cite{barboza2025mean} there exists $\xi\in (x_1,x_2)$ such that
\[
f(x_2) - f(x_1) = f'(\xi)(x_2 - x_1).
\]
Taking absolute values,
\[
|f(x_2) - f(x_1)| = |f'(\xi)|\,|x_2 - x_1|.
\]
There exists $k\in (0,1)$ such that any $x_1$ and $x_2$ we choose
\[
|f'(\xi)|\,|x_2 - x_1|[\le k\,|x_2 - x_1|.
\]

Thus, $f(x)$ is contracting. By the Banach fixed point theorem \cite{BanachFixedPointTheo},
there exists one single fixed point $x_0 \in D$, such that for every $x \in D$, $f^n(x)$ converges to $x_0$ as $n \to \infty$. So the $2$ conditions for $f(x)$ to converge to $x_0$ are

\begin{equation}
\exists \,x_0\in D \text{ such that } f(x_0)=x_0 
\label{Cond1fx=x}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
|f'(x)|<1, x\in D
\label{Cond2absderriv}
\end{equation}

\hfill Q.E.D.


\subsection{Basin Detection Method (BDM)}
\label{process}
Now, we will come up with a method that we will use to find the attracting points and their ranges of convergence for any given function $f(x)$. 
\begin{enumerate}
    \label{refine}
    \item Optional refinement: applicable when $R\subsetneq D$. After the first iteration, we get $R$ as the new domain for the second iteration. This means that the entire domain gets concentrated inside $R$. For that reason, if the convergence is true for $f$ on the domain $R$, it will also be true for all domain $D$. For this reason, we can shrink $D$ and consider only $R$. The motivation for doing this lies in increasing the basin of attraction near fixed points.
    
   % we could directly apply the method on $f^n(x)$ instead of analyzing $f^0(x)$. It is valid, as recursively applying a function to itself does not alter the intervals that converge to a certain fixed point. On practice, this refinement can largely increases the basin of attraction for some functions and it is useful when $D$ contracts to a much smaller set $R$. The basin of attraction that we find could be bigger if we consider big values of $n$. This is the main reason to apply this refinement.%
    \item Solve $|f'(x)|<1$ and recall $D$ as the domain satisfying this equation. Now $f(x)$ satisfies the condition \ref{Cond2absderriv}. $D$ is made up of a set of $n$ continuous intervals: $D=\{I_1,I_2, \dots , I_n\}$, $n\in \mathbb{N}$. The next step could be combined with this step requiring to solve a system of equation, but they can also be computed separately. 

    \item If possible, solve the equation $f(x) = x$ to find all the fixed points. The solution for this equation will be $m$ points with $m\in \mathbb{N},\ m\leq n$. If the equation is hard to solve, draw the line $x=y$ and find its $m$ intercepts with $f(x)$. Note that we have already applied the slope condition \ref{Cond2absderriv}, so each of the fixed points will also be an attracting point. In the case that $m=0$, the function does not converge to any value.
    \item Eliminate from $D$ all the intervals that do not contain a fixed point. We are left with $D=\{I_1,I_2, \dots , I_m\}$.
\end{enumerate}


For each interval $I_k$ of $D$, $1\leq k \leq m$, the domain is ${D}_k$ and the range - ${R}_k$. Note that $|f'(x)| < 1$ for all $x\in I_k$, and there is a fixed point $x_k\in I_k$. These are exactly the $2$ conditions we needed so $f^n(x)$ converges to $x_k$ as proven at \ref{MainProof}. Therefore, $f(x)$ has $m$ different attracting points and each interval $I_k$ converges to $x_k$. This process could be applied to any continuous and differentiable function to find the stable points and their basins of attraction. Figure \ref{visualP} shows an example of the visual application of our BDM on a polynomial function.

