\subsection{Example 2: linear rational functions}


let's look at another interesting family of functions. $f(x)=\dfrac{ax+b}{cx+d}$ where $a,b,c,d \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ and $ad>bc$. The general case just requires looking at another symmetrical case. let's first understand the asymptotic behavior of this function:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{x\to\left(-\frac{d}{c}\right)^-} f(x)
&=
\frac{\frac{bc-ad}{c}}{0^-}
=+\infty,\\[6pt]
\lim_{x\to\left(-\frac{d}{c}\right)^+} f(x)
&=
\frac{\frac{bc-ad}{c}}{0^+}
=-\infty.
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\lim_{x\to\pm\infty} f(x)
=
\lim_{x\to\pm\infty}\frac{a+\frac{b}{x}}{c+\frac{d}{x}}
=\frac{a}{c}.
\]

let's compute the derivative to understand the monotonicity:
\[f'(x)=\dfrac{a(cx+d)-c(ax+b)}{(cx+d)^2}=\dfrac{ad-bc}{(cx+d)^2}\] As $ad>bc$, $f'(x)>0$. So the function is monotonously increasing from $a/c$ to $+\infty$ on the interval $(-\infty,-\dfrac{d}{c})$ and then monotonously increasing from $-\infty$ to $a/c$ on the interval $(-\dfrac{d}{c},+\infty)$ with a vertical asymptote at $x=-d/c$. We can graph our conclusions as shown on the figure \ref{rational}.


