    \label{rational}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection*{Application of the BDM}
Now, let's carefully apply our BDM taking into account the asymptotic behavior of the function. %Note that for the rational functions the domain $D_1$ and range $R_1$ are infinitely large. %Seemingly this is a contradiction to apply our refinement \ref{refine}, but a clever change in the order of instructions will still let us increase the basin of attraction. let's apply our method \ref{process}.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item As $f$ has a vertical and a horizontal asymptote, $D$  and $R$ are infinitely big, there is no contraction so we cannot apply the refinement.
    \item let's solve $|f'(x)|=\bigg|\dfrac{ad-bc}{(cx+d)^2}\bigg|<1$. As $(cx+d)^2\geq0$, \[|ad-bc|<(cx+d)^2 \implies \sqrt{|ad-bc|}<|cx+d|.\] To solve this inequality we can look at both absolute value cases separately:
\\1. $cx+d\geq0 \implies \sqrt{|ad-bc|}<cx+d \implies \dfrac{\sqrt{|ad-bc|}-d}{c}<x$ 
\\2. $cx+d<0 \implies \sqrt{|ad-bc|}<-(cx+d) \implies  -\sqrt{|ad-bc|}>cx+d \\ \implies x<\dfrac{-\sqrt{|ad-bc|}-d}{c}$
Therefore, the answer is the union of the $2$ possibilities:
\[D\in \left(-\infty,\dfrac{-\sqrt{|ad-bc|}-d}{c}\right)\cup \left(\dfrac{\sqrt{|ad-bc|}-d}{c}, +\infty \right).\] let's call $I_1$ and $I_2$ the first and the second intervals respectfully. As we may notice, $D$ can be interpreted as $(-\infty,\dfrac{-d}{c}-k)\cup (\dfrac{-d}{c}+k, +\infty)$, for a constant $k$. This is logical as the derivative explodes to infinity around $x=-d/{c}$ and the intervals are symmetrical with respect to the vertical asymptote. Let $P_1=\dfrac{-d}{c}-k$ and $P_2=\dfrac{-d}{c}+k$. The figure \ref{2st} represents the function after restricting the domain to $D$.



