As the function is monotonously increasing, the inverse is also monotonously increasing \cite{Rudin}. For this reason, The lower bound is
 \[a(\min(x_0))=a(e^{-1})=e^\frac{\ln(e^{-1})}{e^{-1}}=e^\frac{-1}{e^{-1}}=e^{-e}.\]
And the upper bound is
\[a(\max(x_0))=a(e)=e^\frac{1}{e}.\]

The power towers ${}^{\infty}a$ converges on a very specific interval with the bounds tightly related to $e$. Recursion has simplified an infinitely long problem to a problem asking to find an attracting point. Not only this, but we can now find the basin of attraction around $x_0$. When does $f^n(x)$ converge. The meaning of power towers for this is the following: When does ${a^{a^{\cdots\ ^{a^b}}}}\to x_0$? Let's find this interval applying the condition \ref{Cond2absderriv}.
   \[f'(x)=\ln(a)a^x\implies |\ln(a)a^x|<1.\] Observe that $a^x>0$ as $a>0$. Therefore the sign of the value inside the absolute value depends on the sign of $\ln a$. When $a>1$, $\ln a\geq0$ and when $a<1$, $\ln a<0$. Now we can get rid of the absolute value analyzing $2$ different cases:
\[
\begin{cases}
a > 1 \implies \ln(a)\, a^x < 1 
\implies a^x < \frac{1}{\ln(a)}, 
\\[1mm]
a < 1 \implies -\ln(a)\, a^x < 1 
\implies a^x < \frac{1}{-\ln(a)} 
\end{cases}
\]
\[
\implies
\begin{cases}
 \ln(\ln(a)) < -x \ln(a)\implies x < \dfrac{-\ln(\ln(a))}{\ln(a)},\\[1mm]
 \ln(-\ln(a)) < -x \ln(a)\implies x > \dfrac{-\ln(-\ln(a))}{\ln(a)}.
\end{cases}
\]

In summary, when $a\in (1,e^{1/e})$, ${a^{a^{\cdots\ ^{a^b}}}}$ converges to $x_0(a)=e^{-W(-\ln(a))}$ for
\[b\in\left(-\infty, \dfrac{-\ln(\ln(a))}{\ln(a)}\right).\]

And when $a\in (e^{-e},1)$, ${a^{a^{\cdots\ ^{a^b}}}}$ converges to $x_0(a)=e^{-W(-\ln(a))}$ for 
\[b\in\left(\dfrac{-\ln(-\ln(a))}{\ln(a)}, +\infty\right).\]


