Once we leave the real world we face the problem of infinitely many solutions. To begin, let's solve the simplest complex tetration in order to illustrate the point: $z={}^2i=i^i$.
\[i=e^{i(\pi/2+2\pi k)}\implies z=e^{i(\pi/2+2\pi k)\cdot i}=e^{-(\pi/2+2\pi k)}=\boxed{e^{-\pi/2}e^{-2\pi k}}\]

For different values of $k$ we get infinitely many solutions. This was only a power tower of height $2$. And we want to find a power tower of infinite height, so there will be even more solutions. For convenience we will only handle the single solution for $k=0$.


