\section{Conclusion}

In this paper we have achieved our goal, exploring the recursive behavior of the functions in the limit. Answering the research question, we discovered that a single attracting point is necessary and sufficient for a function to converge. We came up with a general method to apply to any function and find its attracting points and basins of attractions. This process was hard to deduce in a general way to include all type of functions, as some functions could have multiple attracting points simultaneously. This implies a restriction to study the local instead of global convergence. Then we applied these conditions on real functions where we used a handy domain contraction idea to maximize the basin of attraction. 

The exponential function led us to discover which of the power towers converged applying the recursive thinking. Finally, we took an insight into the complex tetration, which would be impossible to deal with without the conditions we deduced for real functions. We found out that our conditions continued working for complex numbers, although we had to watch out for the multiplicity of answers. On our way we had to introduce the lambert function and famous theorems as the Mean Value Theorem and the Banach Fixed Point Theorem. It was also very helpful to plot the resulting graphs in Desmos as a visual support for recursive functions. This way we could clearly see an intuitive demonstration for the algebraic equalities.

Although we have found a convergence range for rational functions, we did not prove convergence on all domain. This is a goal for a future paper. We could also find a precise formula describing the basin of attraction for any complex exponential function.

We have explored the functions at their limits, and found out that many functions do have intervals or regions that behave in a stable way at their recursive limit. A single attracting point is necessary for the complex power tower to converge.


